Version: NAFATH-V1
Track: Software

## Overview
A national digital login service must combine security, usability, and legal compliance across websites and mobile applications. OpenID Connect enables applications to verify users’ identities, while OAuth 2.0 supports delegated access to protected resources. Artificial intelligence can identify suspicious login attempts, but its accuracy, bias, privacy risks, and security must be assessed before deployment. Android runtime permissions limit access to sensitive device features and require user approval when applicable. DNS over HTTPS protects DNS queries in transit, but it does not secure all online activity. Phishing-resistant authentication helps protect citizens from fraudulent login pages. The authority must also handle personal information in accordance with applicable Omani data protection requirements. For the Software track, a key next step is verifying that OpenID Connect identity tokens are correctly validated across government services.

## Software
OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework that allows applications to obtain limited access to protected resources. OpenID Connect builds on OAuth 2.0 and adds an identity layer that enables applications to verify a user’s identity and receive basic profile information. For the national login service, OpenID Connect is appropriate for user authentication, while OAuth 2.0 supports delegated access to government resources. Using the correct protocols helps separate identity verification from access permissions.

Resource: OpenID Connect Core 1.0 - https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html
## AI
The NIST AI Risk Management Framework identifies four functions: Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage. Before an AI model flags suspicious logins, the authority should identify threats, assess privacy and bias risks, test detection accuracy, and monitor false positives and false negatives. It should also define human oversight and accountability for decisions that affect citizens. Continuous monitoring is necessary because attackers and login patterns can change over time.

Resource: NIST AI Risk Management Framework Core - https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/
## Mobile
Android runtime permissions restrict access to sensitive features and data, such as location or the camera, when an app targets the relevant protected resources. The app should request a dangerous permission when the user starts a feature that needs it, rather than requesting every permission at launch. Users can deny or revoke permissions, so the login service should explain why access is needed and provide alternatives when possible. A login app should request only permissions essential to its functions.

Resource: Request runtime permissions - Android Developers - https://developer.android.com/training/permissions/requesting
## Networking
DNS over HTTPS (DoH) sends DNS queries and responses over an HTTPS connection, helping protect them from being read or modified by parties monitoring the network path. For the national login service, DoH can improve the privacy and integrity of domain lookups. However, it does not encrypt all website activity, hide the user’s IP address from every party, or protect against phishing and compromised devices. Other security controls remain necessary.

Resource: RFC 8484 - DNS Queries over HTTPS (DoH) - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8484
## Cybersecurity
Phishing-resistant authentication prevents an attacker from successfully reusing authentication data captured by a fake login page. FIDO2 passkeys and security keys can provide phishing-resistant authentication when correctly implemented because they bind authentication to the legitimate website’s origin. By contrast, SMS codes, email codes, and manually entered one-time passwords are not phishing-resistant. The authority should prefer origin-bound authenticators to protect citizens against credential theft and fraudulent login pages.

Resource: NIST SP 800-63B - Authentication and Lifecycle Management - https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b/authenticators/
## Data
Oman’s Personal Data Protection Law, issued by Royal Decree 6/2022, establishes requirements for processing personal data. The authority should verify the law’s scope and any applicable government-specific provisions, provide required privacy information, establish a lawful basis for processing, and apply appropriate security safeguards. It should also check the executive regulation for relevant consent, data-subject rights, retention, and breach-handling requirements. Collecting only necessary information and restricting access helps reduce privacy risks.

Resource: Oman Ministry of Justice and Legal Affairs - Personal Data Protection Law, Royal Decree 6/2022 - https://mjla.gov.om/
## Misconception in My Track
A common misconception is that OAuth 2.0 alone is a user authentication protocol. In fact, OAuth 2.0 is primarily an authorization framework that allows applications to access protected resources with permission. OpenID Connect adds an identity layer on top of OAuth 2.0, enabling a client to verify a user’s identity through an ID Token. Therefore, a national login service should use OpenID Connect for user sign-in rather than treating an OAuth access token as proof of identity. The “Introduction” section of the OpenID Connect Core 1.0 specification explains that OpenID Connect is an identity layer on top of OAuth 2.0.
